course: language
lesson: 46
title: Which number counts? Hyperbole and real numbers in maths
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Word problems and real life are full of numbers, but not every number is there to be counted. «Tom has told his brother a hundred times to tidy up. His brother tidies 3 shelves with 4 books on each» asks about books, and only 3 and 4 are about books.
explain: A real number usually sounds plain and often comes with a unit or a source: «3.2 km on the step-counter», «Everest is 8,849 metres high», «6 jobs on the board». A fake number is round and huge and comes inside a moan or a boast: «a million miles», «a thousand sweets», «a hundred things to do».
explain: Small numbers can be fake too: Mum's «two minutes, love» in a shop doorway often means ten or fifteen.
explain: And when someone corrects themselves — «like a million people, well, thirty» — the last real number is the one that counts.
explain: So before you calculate, ask: which numbers are about the thing the question asks, and which are just feelings?
fact: In a word problem, use only the numbers that are about the thing asked; «told his brother a hundred times» is decoration, and 3 shelves × 4 books = 12 books.
fact: «Sara walked a million miles round the shops» means she walked a long way and is tired; her 2 bags × 5 apples = 10 apples.
fact: What you can count beats what someone says: if Ben says «I've got like a thousand sweets» and empties 14 onto the table, sharing with Ama gives 7 each.
fact: «I want a hundred and ten per cent from every one of you!» means «try your very hardest»; a hundred per cent is all of something, and you cannot give more than all.
fact: «Two minutes, love» from someone chatting in a shop doorway plays the time down; it means «a short while, be patient».
fact: If the board says «Jobs today» with 6 things on it and the teacher sighs «I've got a hundred things to do», he has 6 jobs and feels very busy.
fact: «Not in a million years, sunshine» means «never»; «sunshine» said like that is firm and half-joking, not pleased.
fact: «A one in a million chance» means «very rare»; for four-leaf clovers, the estimates usually given are nearer one in five or ten thousand.
fact: «We walked for miles and miles» is a feeling; «We walked about three kilometres» is a measurement, and in a maths answer you use the measurement.
fact: When someone corrects themselves, the last real number counts: «like a million people, well, thirty» means 30.
fact: Exact numbers with units from a teacher or a book (Everest is 8,849 metres high) are real; round huge numbers in a moan («that hill's a thousand miles high») are not.
exercise: «Tom has told his brother a hundred times to tidy up. His brother tidies 3 shelves with 4 books on each. How many books did he tidy?» | 12 | 1200 | 107 | 40
exercise: In Tom's problem, which numbers are about the books? | 3 and 4 only | 100 and 3 only | 100, 3 and 4 | 100 and 4 only
exercise: «Sara walked a million miles round the shops! She bought 2 bags with 5 apples in each. How many apples?» | 10 | 7 | 2 million | 1 million and 10
exercise: What does «a million miles round the shops» tell you? | Sara walked a long way and is tired | Sara walked exactly a million miles | The problem is broken and can't be done | Each apple travelled a million miles
exercise: Ben says «I've got like a thousand sweets», then empties his pocket: 14 sweets. He shares equally with Ama. How many does each get? | 7 | 500 | 14 | 28
exercise: The coach at half time: «I want a hundred and ten per cent!» What does he want? | Everyone to try their very hardest | Each player to score ten more goals | Ten more players to come on the pitch | The team to stop playing for a while
exercise: Why can't you really give a hundred and ten per cent? | A hundred per cent is already all of it | Coaches are not allowed to ask for it | A hundred and ten is an odd number | Per cent only works for money and prices in shops
exercise: Mum in the shop doorway: «Two minutes, love, I'll be two minutes». How long will she probably be? | A while, maybe ten or fifteen minutes | Exactly two minutes on the clock | Two whole hours, because she loves to chat | No time, because she's coming now
exercise: Mr Okafor sighs: «I've got a hundred things to do today». The board says «Jobs today» with 6 things. How many jobs does he have? | 6 | 100 | 106 | 94
exercise: Dad says «Not in a million years, sunshine» about a pet snake. What does he mean? | No, and not any time soon | Yes, in a million years' time | Maybe, if the sun is shining | Yes, he's pleased
exercise: Maya finds a four-leaf clover: «That's a one in a million chance!» What does she mean? | It is rare and lucky | Scientists measured exactly one in a million | It will happen to a million people | She found loads
exercise: Kai says «We walked for miles and miles!». Freya says «We walked about three kilometres». The app says 3.2 km. Which do you use in a maths answer? | Freya's three kilometres | Kai's miles and miles | Half of each of them | Neither, because they argue
exercise: Aoife says «There were like a million people there, well, thirty, but it was mad busy». How many people were there? | 30 | 1,000,000 | 1,000,030 | 31
exercise: Which height is a measured fact? | «Everest is 8,849 metres high», said by the teacher | «That hill's a thousand miles high», said by Dad | Both are measured facts | Neither is a measured fact
exercise: What does Josh's dad really mean by «That hill's a thousand miles high, I'm not walking up it»? | The hill is steep and tiring | The hill is higher than Everest | He has measured the hill | He wants to climb it twice
exercise: Before you calculate, what question should you ask about each number? | Is it about the thing the question asks? | Is it the biggest number in the problem? | Is it written in words or in digits? | Was it said first or was it said last?
test: «miss it says a hundred times, so is it 100 × 3 × 4??» What do you say? | No, the hundred is how often he nagged; only 3 and 4 are books | Yes, every number in a problem must be used somewhere | No, it should be 100 + 3 + 4 = 107 books | Yes, because the biggest number always goes first
test: Ben brags «a thousand sweets» and gives Ama «half». Ama expects 500. What really happens? | She gets 7, because there were only 14 | She gets 500, because half of a thousand is 500 | She gets 14, because Ben gives her all of them | She gets nothing, because there were no sweets
test: «coach wants 110% lol so if I usually run 10 laps I gotta do 11 then?» What is right? | No, he just means «try your hardest»; it's not a sum | Yes, exactly 11 laps and not one lap more | Yes, and the goalie must do 110 laps | No, he means you should do 110 laps
test: Mum said «two minutes» in the shop doorway. After two minutes you shout «you SAID two!». What have you missed? | «Two minutes» meant «a short while», so be patient | Mum can't tell the time on her phone | Two minutes is really longer than ten minutes, so she's early | Mum wanted to go home the long way round
test: «Dad said not in a million years so I'll ask again in a million years innit» What did Dad really mean? | Never, or not any time soon: it's a clear no | Yes, but only as soon as a million years have passed | Ask again tomorrow and he might say yes | Only if you buy the snake yourself
test: «it's a one in a million clover so if I look at a million I'll find one, easy» What is wrong? | «One in a million» just meant «rare»; the real chance is much better | Nothing, one in a million is the exact chance that scientists measured | You need to look at two million to find one | Four-leaf clovers do not exist at all
test: ★★ Aoife: «like a million people, well, thirty». Rosie: «a million Robux, well, 500». What rule works for both? | The last, corrected number counts | The first number said always counts | The biggest number always counts | Add the two numbers together
test: ★★ In a problem: «Dad's had a thousand cups of tea today, ugh. He made 4 cups for each of 3 guests.» How many cups did he make for guests? | 12 | 1000 | 1012 | 7
test: ★★★ «We walked like a million miles!» The app says the class walked 3.2 km there and the same back. How far did they walk in all? | 6.4 km | 3.2 km | A million miles | 2 million miles
test: ★★★ Mr Okafor moans about «a hundred things to do». His board shows 6 jobs; he finishes half before lunch and 1 after. How many jobs are left? | 2 | 3 | 99 | 50
test: ★★★ «There were like a thousand kids at the party, no, twenty, oh and five grown-ups.» Each person got 2 balloons. How many balloons in all? | 50 | 40 | 2,000 | 2,010
exam: It is given that Tom «has told his brother a hundred times» to tidy, and that the brother tidies three shelves each bearing four books. Determine the aggregate number of books tidied. | Twelve | One thousand two hundred | One hundred and seven | Forty
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the figure «one hundred and ten per cent», as employed by a sports coach, cannot denote a quantity. | One hundred per cent constitutes the whole, and no part may exceed the whole | The figure is an odd number and therefore cannot be divided | Percentages may be applied solely to sums of money | Coaches are prohibited by regulation from employing percentages
exam: A speaker states «a million people, well, thirty». Ascertain the number of persons present. | Thirty | One million | One million and thirty | It cannot be ascertained
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the figures «8,849 metres», stated by a teacher in respect of Everest, and «a thousand miles», asserted by a parent in respect of a local hill? | The former is a measurement; the latter conveys a feeling of fatigue | Both are measurements of equal reliability | The former is an exaggeration; the latter is a measurement | Neither figure may be regarded as a measurement
